# Decibyl — deployment environment
#
# Copy to the repository root as `.env` (or append the sections you need to the
# `.env` that `scripts/setup_remote.sh` writes) and fill in everything marked
# CHANGE ME.
#
# **Quote every value containing a space, comma, `<`, `>` or `&`.** This file is
# read with `source`, by root, during deployment preflight — an unquoted
#     SUPPLIER_ADDRESS=No.86/16, Papanna Thottam, ...
# sets the variable to `No.86/16,` and then tries to run `Papanna` as a command.
# The address and legal-name lines below are already quoted for that reason.
#
# The api container is given this whole file (see `env_file:` in
# docker-compose.yaml), except for the values the compose file computes from
# container hostnames — DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL and the MinIO endpoint, which it
# always wins on. Adding a setting here needs no other edit.

# ─── Written by scripts/setup_remote.sh — do not hand-edit ──────────────────
# ENVIRONMENT, SERVER_IP, PUBLIC_HOST, PUBLIC_BASE_URL, TURN_SECRET,
# FASTAPI_WORKERS, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, REDIS_PASSWORD, OSS_JWT_SECRET.
# Every re-run of remote_up.sh rewrites those four host/URL keys and leaves
# everything else in this file untouched.

# ─── Secrets you generate once ──────────────────────────────────────────────

# Encrypts platform provider API keys at rest. **Without it, saving a provider
# key raises and no call can be placed at all.** Generate with:
#   python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
# Changing it later makes every stored key undecryptable — they must be re-entered.
PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL_SECRET="CHANGE ME"

# ─── Razorpay ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Test and live mode have separate keys *and separate webhook secrets*. Create
# the webhook in the Razorpay dashboard for the mode you are in:
#   URL     https://<your-host>/api/v1/billing/razorpay/webhook
#   Events  payment.captured, payment.failed
#   Secret  you choose it — paste the identical string below
#
# Without RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET top-ups are refused outright, deliberately:
# the alternative is charging a customer and crediting nobody.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID="CHANGE ME"
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET="CHANGE ME"
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET="CHANGE ME"

# ─── Autopay for the monthly number rental ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Razorpay **Subscriptions** is a separate product from the one-off orders
# above and needs its own activation on the account — their approval, not our
# configuration, and it can take days. Start it before you need it.
#
# Add these events to the same webhook: subscription.activated,
# subscription.charged, subscription.halted, subscription.pending,
# subscription.cancelled.
#
# Create the ₹349 monthly plan once and pin it here. Left unset, the first
# mandate creates a plan on the fly and logs a warning — workable, but a plan
# created per environment fragments Razorpay's own reporting and the fragments
# are indistinguishable afterwards.
# RAZORPAY_RENTAL_PLAN_ID=plan_CHANGEME

# Whether a number may only be issued against an authorised mandate. **On by
# default**, because the alternative is the failure the whole managed-number
# path exists to prevent: a number we pay a carrier for every month, attached
# to an account whose prepaid balance ran out, with nothing standing behind the
# rent.
#
# Set it false *only* while Subscriptions activation is still pending — with it
# false the rental falls back to the prepaid balance and the dunning schedule
# (day 7 suspend, day 45 release-eligible), which works but leans on the
# customer remembering to top up.
# REQUIRE_MANDATE_FOR_NUMBERS=false

# ─── Who is supplying, for the top half of every invoice ────────────────────
#
# Facts about one company, not per-customer settings. Missing values do not
# roll back a payment — the credit lands and the failure to issue a document is
# logged as an error, because losing a customer's money over a config gap is
# the worse outcome.
SUPPLIER_LEGAL_NAME="NAUTOMATION LABS PRIVATE LIMITED"
SUPPLIER_GSTIN=33AALCN7211L1ZB
SUPPLIER_ADDRESS="No.86/16, Papanna Thottam, Brindhavan Nagar, TNHB PH-7, Hosur - 635109, Krishnagiri District, Tamil Nadu"

# Zero-rated export of services under a Letter of Undertaking. With
# SUPPLIER_HAS_LUT=false every foreign customer's top-up is refused rather than
# charged 18% they should not be paying.
SUPPLIER_HAS_LUT=true
SUPPLIER_LUT_NUMBER=AB3306264934610

# SUPPLIER_STATE_CODE defaults to the first two digits of the GSTIN — 33, Tamil
# Nadu. It decides CGST+SGST versus IGST for every domestic customer, so an
# incorrect value produces the right total split the wrong way: a filing
# correction rather than a visible bug. Set it explicitly only if you know the
# derivation is wrong.
# SUPPLIER_STATE_CODE=33

# ─── Outbound email ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Plain SMTP, so any provider works: SES, Sendgrid, Mailgun, Postmark, or a
# Workspace/Gmail account with an app password.
#
# Two things use it, and the second is the one that is easy to skip and
# expensive to have skipped:
#
#   * receipt vouchers and tax invoices, emailed to the billing contact
#   * the low-balance warning, sent daily at 09:00 IST
#
# Without the warning the dunning schedule suspends a number seven days after
# a rental it could not collect, in silence — the money was almost always
# there and nobody said it was needed.
#
# Leave SMTP_HOST unset to skip email entirely. Nothing raises for want of it:
# documents still issue and stay downloadable from the billing screen, and the
# billing jobs still bill. A job whose real work is money must not fail
# because a courtesy could not be sent, so the low-balance job logs one line a
# day saying it is off instead.
#
# SMTP_USE_TLS means "use TLS", and the PORT decides which kind: 465 gets
# implicit TLS, anything else gets STARTTLS. So it belongs ON for both common
# setups — 587 with STARTTLS and 465 with implicit TLS — and OFF only for a
# plaintext relay you host yourself.
#
# It used to say the opposite here: that 587 "negotiates STARTTLS" on its own,
# so the flag could stay false. It does not. STARTTLS is only issued when this
# is true (see api/services/messaging/email.py), and a false value on 587 opens
# a plaintext session that every hosted provider refuses — Resend answers
# `538 Must issue a STARTTLS command first`, which reads like a broken
# credential rather than a wrong flag.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USERNAME=resend
# SMTP_PASSWORD=CHANGE ME
# SMTP_USE_TLS=true

# The From address has to be one the SMTP service has verified, or every
# message is dropped silently at their end rather than ours.
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=billing@decibyl.ai
# EMAIL_FROM_NAME=Decibyl

# ─── Data protection ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DPDP s13 requires a named, contactable person for data protection
# grievances, published where a Data Principal can find it. Served at
# GET /api/v1/privacy/subprocessors so the app and the website cannot drift
# apart on who it is. A name is required — a role mailbox alone is not a person.
GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_NAME="CHANGE ME"
GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_EMAIL=privacy@decibyl.ai
GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_ADDRESS="No.86/16, Papanna Thottam, Brindhavan Nagar, TNHB PH-7, Hosur - 635109, Krishnagiri District, Tamil Nadu"

# Defaults are 90 and 365. Audio and text age separately on purpose: a
# recording is a person's voice and is rarely useful a month later, while a
# transcript is what reporting actually reads. Customers can set their own
# shorter windows in the product.
# DEFAULT_RECORDING_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# DEFAULT_TRANSCRIPT_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# Spoken before the greeting on every call. On by default; only an explicit
# `false` turns it off, and doing so is a decision to make deliberately.
# RECORDING_DISCLOSURE_ENABLED=true
# RECORDING_DISCLOSURE_TEXT="Just so you know, this call is recorded for quality and training purposes."

# Follow the caller's language mid-call.
#
# An agent is configured with one language; a caller in India frequently is not
# — they open in English, switch to Hindi when it gets substantive, and mix both
# in a sentence. With this on, the agent's voice follows after two consecutive
# turns in the new language, so a single mis-detection cannot flip it.
#
# Needs an STT in multilingual mode to be useful. Deepgram already defaults to
# "multi", so detection has been running all along with nothing reading it.
# Realtime speech-to-speech models ignore this — they hear the caller directly
# and already answer in the language they hear.
#
# Off by default: it changes what a caller hears mid-conversation, which is not
# a thing to switch on for an account without telling them.
# FOLLOW_CALLER_LANGUAGE=true

# **Leave MINIO_PUBLIC_BUCKET unset.** `true` grants the recordings bucket
# anonymous read, write *and* delete. Recordings are served by presigned URL and
# need no bucket policy at all.

# ─── Managed phone numbers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Decibyl's own Plivo account. Compliance applications are filed and managed
# numbers are bought under *this* account, never a customer's — a customer's
# credentials on a telephony configuration are for their own numbers only.
#
# Without these, forwarding a KYC application raises rather than quietly
# falling back to "a human will handle it", which is how applications sit
# unsent for a fortnight.
# PLATFORM_PLIVO_AUTH_ID=CHANGE ME
# PLATFORM_PLIVO_AUTH_TOKEN=CHANGE ME

# The Plivo Application whose answer_url points at our inbound dispatcher.
# Numbers are bought with this app_id set, so there is no console step and no
# window where a number is rented but answers nowhere.
# PLATFORM_PLIVO_APPLICATION_ID=CHANGE ME

# What a number costs us per month, and what we charge for it, both in paise.
# Two numbers, not one: recording only the retail price is how a margin figure
# starts lying. Confirm the cost against Plivo's live India price list — the
# default is the launch-plan estimate, not a quote.
# NUMBER_RENTAL_COST_PAISE=25000
# NUMBER_RENTAL_PRICE_PAISE=49900

# Opens telephony verification to customers. Leave false until the Plivo
# reseller arrangement is approved: it gates document upload, and collecting
# identity records we cannot forward takes on DPDP custody for nothing.
# MANAGED_TELEPHONY_ENABLED=true

# ─── Trial calling and number verification ──────────────────────────────────

# How a verification code reaches the person holding the phone.
#
#   log        development only — writes the code to the log and refuses to
#              run outside a dev ENVIRONMENT. The default, so a deployment
#              that has configured nothing fails visibly rather than
#              appearing to send codes it never sent.
#   voice      call the number and read the code out. Dials from the shared
#              outbound pool, so it needs at least one number in that pool
#              (Superadmin → Shared outbound numbers) and nothing else.
#   plivo_sms  SMS on PLATFORM_PLIVO_*, and twilio_sms on PLATFORM_TWILIO_*.
#              Both wait on DLT registration for Indian destinations, which
#              voice does not — unregistered SMS is dropped by the operator
#              and the failure looks like success.
#
# This is the setting that decides whether a trial account can place a test
# call at all. An account with no carrier of its own falls back to the shared
# pool, and that fallback requires a verified destination whatever
# REQUIRE_VERIFIED_TEST_NUMBER says — so with the channel left at `log`, the
# pool is configured and every trial call is still refused.
# VERIFICATION_CHANNEL=voice

# The sender number for platform-originated SMS, on Decibyl's own account.
# Must be the number registered against the DLT header. SMS channels only.
# PLATFORM_SMS_FROM_NUMBER=CHANGE ME

# Decibyl's own Twilio account, the alternative to PLATFORM_PLIVO_* for
# platform-originated SMS. Having both is about not being locked to one
# carrier — it is NOT a way around DLT, which attaches to the sending entity
# and the Indian destination rather than to the carrier.
# PLATFORM_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=CHANGE ME
# PLATFORM_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=CHANGE ME

# Whether a test call may only go to a number the account has verified. Off by
# default because a permission nobody can obtain is not a permission, it is an
# outage — turn it on in the same change that sets VERIFICATION_CHANNEL to
# something deliverable. Does not affect campaigns or the trigger API.
# REQUIRE_VERIFIED_TEST_NUMBER=true

# ─── Hostnames ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Setting DECIBYL_APP_HOST is what switches the deployment from one hostname to
# four — decibyl-init renders the subdomain nginx config on the next `up`.
# Leave it unset to keep the single-host config.
#
# It also becomes UI_APP_URL, which is what the **embed widget snippet** points
# at. Left to its localhost default, every snippet a customer pastes into their
# own site loads a script from localhost and silently does nothing on their
# visitors' machines.
# DECIBYL_APP_HOST=app.decibyl.ai
# DECIBYL_API_HOST=api.decibyl.ai
# DECIBYL_DOCS_HOST=docs.decibyl.ai
# DECIBYL_ROOT_HOST=decibyl.ai

# Only needed if the app is reached somewhere other than https://$DECIBYL_APP_HOST.
# UI_APP_URL=https://app.decibyl.ai

# ─── While you are testing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Prepaid is enforced by default and a fresh account has zero credit, so with
# this left on your own test account cannot place a single call. Turn it back
# on before anyone else signs up.
BALANCE_ENFORCEMENT_ENABLED=false

# Close registration once your accounts exist. Do it *after* creating the first
# admin — nothing in the signup flow sets the staff flag, and with signup closed
# there is no way to create the first account at all.
# ENABLE_SIGNUP=false

# ─── Google OAuth — used by TWO features that share ONE client ──────────────
#
# READ THIS BEFORE REGISTERING THE CLIENT. These two variables are consumed by
# both "Sign in with Google" and the google_calendar tool, and the two features
# have DIFFERENT callbacks. An OAuth client registered with only one of them
# leaves the other failing at Google with redirect_uri_mismatch — an error that
# never reaches our logs, because Google refuses before redirecting to us.
#
# Register an OAuth 2.0 Client (Web application) in Google Cloud Console —
# APIs & Services → Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID — and
# add BOTH of these as Authorised redirect URIs:
#
#   https://<your-API-host>/api/v1/auth/google/callback
#   https://<your-API-host>/api/v1/integrations/google-calendar/callback
#
# Note <your-API-host>, not the app host: the sign-in callback is derived from
# BACKEND_API_ENDPOINT, which on a subdomain deployment is api.<your-domain>.
# Registering the app host instead is the same mismatch by a different route.
#
# Two more things that produce "Google sign-in does nothing":
#
#   * OAuth consent screen still in Testing. Only accounts added under Test
#     users can sign in; everyone else is refused at Google. Publish it, or add
#     the people who need to sign in.
#   * UI_APP_URL unset. The callback hands the browser back to
#     UI_APP_URL/auth/google?token=..., which defaults to http://localhost:3010
#     — so sign-in succeeds and then redirects into nowhere.
#
# Enable the Google Calendar API on the project as well if you use that tool.
# Leave both unset and Google sign-in simply does not render, and the calendar
# tool is unavailable to create. Nothing else depends on them.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=CHANGE ME
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE ME

# IANA zone name events are created in. Google's API takes a zone name, not a
# UTC offset, so this default is correct year-round for an India deployment
# without any DST handling. Override for a deployment outside India.
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_DEFAULT_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata

# ─── Optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
